  ('ff4fec18-6bb9-457e-b127-8826199234fb','Sycamore State Park Archery Range sits inside the park system in Trotwood, Ohio, giving it a different feel than a private club or storefront since it''s operated as a public facility on state land. Public ranges like this tend to draw a mix of casual weekend shooters, families, and hunters sighting in before Ohio''s deer season, without the membership dues or scheduling quirks of a private club. There''s nothing on file here about lane counts, target styles, or seasonal hours, which is common for state park amenities that operate on the park''s own calendar. Anyone planning a visit should check with the park directly for current access rules and whether the range is open that day.'),
  ('8339b63a-df04-4478-a9d5-6a6dc5836411','T.H.E. Archery Club calls Wakarusa, Kansas home, a small town setting where a club like this often serves as the main organized archery outlet for miles around. It''s set up as a volunteer club with a 3D course on site, putting it in line with the bowhunting-practice tradition common across rural Kansas, where foam-animal courses double as off-season tune-up spots ahead of deer season. Beyond the 3D course, there''s no posted detail on membership structure, lane counts, or a lesson program, which is typical for a small-town volunteer operation running on member time rather than a business schedule. Reaching out ahead of a visit is the safest way to confirm the course is open and staffed.'),
  ('db636893-9882-46ba-aff6-349911909270','TAA - Plano, listed under Dallas, Texas, runs as a club that includes lessons in its offerings, which sets it apart from groups that only cater to archers who already know their way around a bow. The Dallas-Fort Worth area has one of the denser archery scenes in the country, with plenty of youth and adult clubs competing for attention, so a lesson-friendly program is a real draw for beginners trying to figure out where to start. Course layout, membership cost, and specific meeting locations aren''t listed here, so the practical move for anyone interested is a direct call or message to confirm when lessons run and what they involve before showing up.'),
  ('6a93b0e2-2dcd-4cef-92ca-888247b505d4','TAC Archery operates out of Crossville, Tennessee, up on the Cumberland Plateau, as a volunteer club that offers lessons alongside its regular shooting activities. That''s a notable pairing for a member-run operation, since teaching newcomers usually takes more organized effort than simply keeping a range open for existing members. Crossville and the surrounding plateau area have a strong outdoors and hunting culture, so a club willing to bring in beginners fills a real gap for locals who want to learn archery without committing to gear and a membership sight unseen. Specifics on lesson scheduling, cost, or course type aren''t posted, so contacting the club directly is the way to get real answers.'),
  ('4c323ddd-45ec-4509-8029-8aac868943c0','Tacoma Sportsmen''s Club in Tacoma, Washington runs as a club offering both a field archery course and lessons, a combination that points to a group serious about traditional outdoor-format shooting rather than just an indoor lane setup. Field archery, with its varied distances and often wooded terrain, has a loyal following in the Pacific Northwest, and pairing that course with a lesson program gives newer archers a structured way into a discipline that can otherwise feel intimidating to pick up alone. Puget Sound archers looking for something beyond a flat indoor range have a real option here. Membership terms and lesson scheduling aren''t detailed, so reaching out directly is the best way to get current specifics.'),
  ('95d8f87e-0937-4084-9f44-be844717066e','Tall Tine Bowstrings & Pro Shop in Castlewood, South Dakota combines a commercial pro shop with a 3D archery course, giving it a dual identity as both a retail stop for gear and string work and a place to actually shoot foam-animal targets on site. That pairing is common in rural areas where a single business ends up serving as the de facto community archery hub, since there''s rarely enough demand to support a shop and a separate club. Eastern South Dakota has a strong bowhunting base, and a pro shop with its own 3D course is well positioned to serve hunters tuning gear ahead of the fall season. Hours and course access details aren''t listed, so a call ahead is worthwhile.'),
  ('e4345655-1333-4d14-a9fa-0bb6c7e942a9','Tangi Archery Club serves the Baton Rouge, Louisiana area as a volunteer-run club, the kind of grassroots operation that keeps archery accessible in a region where commercial ranges are less common than member-organized ones. South Louisiana''s hunting and outdoor culture supports a fair number of these small clubs, often built around a handful of dedicated members maintaining a course and running informal shoots rather than a posted retail schedule. There''s no detail here on course type, lesson availability, or membership cost, which is typical for a club running on volunteer time rather than business hours. Anyone interested in shooting there should reach out to the club directly to find out what''s currently active and how to get involved.'),
  ('79523e25-ba1e-431e-9461-e9a0bde8b1e5','Tangy''s Pro Shop & Indoor Range operates in Warwick, Rhode Island as a commercial business, combining a retail pro shop with an indoor range under one address. Indoor setups like this matter a lot in New England, where winter weather rules out outdoor shooting for a good chunk of the year, and having a shop attached means archers can get gear serviced or restrung without a separate trip. Rhode Island''s archery scene is compact, so a dedicated indoor commercial range in the Providence metro area fills a real niche for local shooters wanting to stay sharp year-round. Lane counts, hours, and pricing aren''t listed here, so calling ahead is the way to confirm what''s currently offered.'),
  ('8271766f-3e30-446e-8c23-366f0b4b7800','Tapp Nation Archery, in Riverview, Florida just southeast of Tampa, runs as a commercial archery business rather than a membership club, the kind of setup built around walk-in customers and drop-in shooting sessions. The greater Tampa Bay area has a decent cluster of archery activity, and a commercial operation in a fast-growing suburb like Riverview is well positioned to catch both newcomers curious about the sport and established archers looking for a closer-to-home option. No specific details on lanes, course type, or lesson offerings are listed for this location, so anyone interested in visiting should call ahead to confirm hours and what shooting options are currently available.'),
  ('9038ff05-e0c5-4804-b4d9-bb79e2518006','Tar River Archery Club, based in Louisburg, North Carolina, is a club serving the area north and east of Raleigh along the Tar River, a part of the state with a solid rural hunting tradition but fewer organized archery options than the bigger metro areas. A club like this often functions as the local gathering point for archers who''d otherwise have to drive well into the Triangle for a course or community. No specifics are posted here about course format, membership requirements, or whether lessons are offered, which is common for smaller regional clubs that rely on word of mouth. Reaching out directly is the best way to find out what''s currently running and how to join.')
) AS v(id, description)
